"""
IP addresses, IPv4 and IPv6: parse, render, mask, match and anonymise.

- An address is a Free value: it copies, compares and sits in a record like a number.
- Nothing here touches the network: no lookup, no socket and no name resolution.
- Parsing is strict and gives None for anything outside the forms each parser names.
- Text comes out in the form RFC 5952 asks for an IPv6 address, and as a dotted quad for IPv4.
- Addresses of different families are never the same and never in one subnet.
- Anonymising cuts an address to its /24 or /48; there is no way back.

    case parseIp(asSpan(&peer)) of
        when Some(value as address: IpAddress) do
            let inside: Bool := inSubnet(address, office, 24);
            let logged: String := ipToString(anonymise(address));
        when None do
            printLn("not an address");
    end case;
"""
pragma Targets(Names => "linux openbsd freebsd darwin wasm");
import Standard.String (
    String,
    length
);

module Standard.Ip is
    """
    An IPv4 or an IPv6 address.  Free, so it copies.
    """
    type IpAddress: Free;

    ---
    --- Parsing
    ---

    """
    A dotted-quad IPv4 address: exactly four decimal groups, each 0 to 255,
    each of one to three digits.  None for anything else -- a fifth group
    is not accepted, and neither is a group above 255.
    No contract: an Option result, which an ensures cannot look into.
    """
    generic [R: Region]
    function parseIpv4(string: Span[Nat8, R]): Option[IpAddress];

    """
    An IPv6 address in the usual text form, with at most one ::.  Groups
    are one to four hexadecimal digits, either case.

    None for anything else.  not accepted, and named rather than left to
    be discovered: the IPv4-mapped text form ::ffff:1.2.3.4, and a zone
    identifier such as %eth0.
    No contract: an Option result, which an ensures cannot look into.
    """
    generic [R: Region]
    function parseIpv6(string: Span[Nat8, R]): Option[IpAddress];

    """
    Either form.  A string holding a colon is tried as IPv6, anything else
    as IPv4.
    No contract: an Option result, which an ensures cannot look into.
    """
    generic [R: Region]
    function parseIp(string: Span[Nat8, R]): Option[IpAddress];

    """
    The address held in bytes, in network byte order: 4 bytes are an IPv4
    address, 16 an IPv6 one.  None for any other length.
    No contract: an Option result, which an ensures cannot look into.
    """
    generic [R: Region]
    function ipFromBytes(bytes: Span[Nat8, R]): Option[IpAddress];

    """
    The address as bytes in network byte order: 4 for an IPv4 address, 16
    for an IPv6 one.  ipFromBytes reads them back.
    """
    function ipToBytes(address: IpAddress): String
        ensures (length(&result) = 4) or (length(&result) = 16);

    ---
    --- Asking
    ---

    """
    Is this an IPv6 address?
    No contract: the answer is a Bool, nothing cheaper holds.
    """
    function isIpv6(address: IpAddress): Bool;

    """
    Are the two the same address?  Two addresses of different families are
    never the same, whatever their bits.
    No contract: the answer is a Bool, nothing cheaper holds.
    """
    function sameIp(left: IpAddress, right: IpAddress): Bool;

    """
    Whether the address is not a public one a request should go to.  For
    IPv4: 0.0.0.0/8, the three private networks of RFC 1918, the shared
    address space of RFC 6598, 127.0.0.0/8, link-local of RFC 3927,
    192.0.0.0/24, 192.0.2.0/24, 198.18.0.0/15, 198.51.100.0/24,
    203.0.113.0/24, and all of 224.0.0.0/4 and 240.0.0.0/4.  For IPv6:
    ::/96, 64:ff9b:1::/48, 100::/64, 2001::/23, 2001:db8::/32, 2002::/16,
    3fff::/20, unique local of RFC 4193, link-local of RFC 4291,
    fec0::/10 and ff00::/8; an address in ::ffff:0:0/96 or in
    64:ff9b::/96 is judged as the IPv4 address in its last 32 bits.
    No contract: the answer is a Bool, nothing cheaper holds.
    """
    function isPrivateOrReserved(address: IpAddress): Bool;

    ---
    --- Rendering
    ---

    """
    The address as text: dotted quad for IPv4, and for IPv6 the form RFC
    5952 asks for -- lower case, no leading zeros in a group, and the
    longest run of two or more zero groups written ::, the leftmost run
    when two are equally long.
    No contract: a text whose length follows its input.
    """
    function ipToString(address: IpAddress): String;

    ---
    --- Masks and matching
    ---

    """
    The address with everything below the top bits bits cleared.

    None when bits is larger than the address has: 32 for IPv4, 128 for
    IPv6.  A mask of 0 gives the all-zero address, which is what it means.
    No contract: an Option result, which an ensures cannot look into.
    """
    function applyMask(address: IpAddress, bits: Index): Option[IpAddress];

    """
    Is address inside the subnet subnet/bits?

    false when the two are of different families, and false when bits
    is too large -- a question that cannot be asked is not answered true.
    The subnet is masked too, so a caller that wrote 198.51.100.3/24 gets
    the answer for 198.51.100.0/24 rather than a silent false.
    No contract: the answer is a Bool, nothing cheaper holds.
    """
    function inSubnet(address: IpAddress, subnet: IpAddress, bits: Index): Bool;

    ---
    --- Anonymising
    ---

    """
    The address with the part that identifies a household removed: the low
    8 bits of an IPv4 address, the low 80 of an IPv6 one.

    That is /24 and /48.  It is a reduction and not
    a hash: two addresses in the same /24 become the same value, and there
    is no way back to the original.
    No contract: a value of its own type built from its arguments; no property cheaper than its type holds.
    """
    function anonymise(address: IpAddress): IpAddress;
end module.
